    "descriptionEn": "Kuk Lbeuk Svay is an ancient temple built in the 12th century. The temple has a two-story wall, the outer wall leaving a small mark about 70 meters long, while the inner wall is strong, about 25x35 meters long. The rest of the gopura is still well buried in the ground for more than a meter. As for the body of the temple, it collapsed, leaving only the main door and some elements of the temple. Inside the inner wall there are traces of a tower and a laterite tower. In 2002, people went to build houses near the temple and cleared the vines that surrounded the temple, and found the old chains (legs) that our ancestors left at the bottom of the temple. It can be estimated that the temple was built during the reign of Jayavarman VII in early 1181 AD. Here there is a pedestal supporting a laterite statue.",
